  "question": {
    "text": "A transistor is used in common-emitter mode in an amplifier circuit. When a signal of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mi>mV</mi></math> is added to the base-emitter voltage, the base current changes by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mi>&#956;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> and the collector current changes by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mA</mi></math>. The load resistance is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi><mi>&#937;</mi></math>. The voltage gain of the transistor will be _____ ."
  },
  "options": [],
  "correct_answer": "750",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>First, use the formula of current gain in terms&nbsp;of&nbsp;change in base current, and collector current,&nbsp;</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#946;</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#916;</mi><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>&#916;</mi><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p>given,&nbsp;</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#8710;</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>,&nbsp;</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8710;</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math></p><p>So the formula of voltage gain,&nbsp;</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#946;</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>&#8710;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>&#8710;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>&#8710;</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>&#215;</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>&#8710;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><msub><mi>&#946;</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>5000</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>750</mn></math></p>"
  }
}